8274 witness -> ERC-1833 advanceCursor: the seam
(concrete first step of the 1833 co-design — layer 2 metering composing onto our proven layers 3+4)
Author: babyblueviper1 (Fede) / trustless-ai. Draft for blockbird + group review.

PRINCIPLE
One concern per side, none overlapping:
- the VERIFIER (8274/8299) produces a recomputable witness  — judges, does not enforce
- the CURSOR (ERC-1833) enforces bounds + draw-once         — counts, does not judge
- the ESCROW (layer 4 / 8275) settles owner-bound           — pays, does not judge
Gate discipline throughout: never on valid alone; multi-condition; replay-nullified.

THE WITNESS (what 8274 hands advanceCursor)
A recomputable, signed assertion that a specific action is verified AND scoped to a capability leaf.

    struct Witness {
        // --- recomputable verdict (8299/WYRIWE receipt) ---
        bytes32   receiptId;     // NIP-01 event id of the signed verdict (kind 30078)
        bytes     receiptProof;  // packReceiptProof(): px, rx, s, preimage  (on-chain BIP-340 verifiable)
        bytes32   artifactHash;  // canonical hash of the action this verdict is OF
        // --- capability binding (which scope, how much) ---
        address   agent;         // the acting agent (8004 identity)
        bytes32   leaf;          // capability leaf = keccak(scopeId, subCap, agentId)
        bytes32[] merkleProof;   // proof leaf in capabilityRoot[agent]
        uint256   amount;        // amount to draw this action
    }

THE GATE (advanceCursor checks ALL — never valid alone)

    function advanceCursor(Witness calldata w) external {
        // (1) VALID — recompute the verdict ON-CHAIN; trust the math, not an oracle
        (bool valid, bool matches) = VERIFIER.verify(w.artifactHash, w.receiptProof);
        require(valid,   "witness: invalid signature");   // 8274 == our deployed BIP340Verifier
        require(matches, "witness: artifact mismatch");   // the verdict is OF this action

        // (2) IN ROOT — bounded authority: leaf is in the agent's accepted capability tree
        require(_verifyMerkle(w.leaf, w.merkleProof, capabilityRoot[w.agent]), "witness: leaf not in root");

        // (3) WITHIN SUBCAP — headroom on this scope's budget
        require(leafSpent[w.leaf] + w.amount <= _subCap(w.leaf), "witness: over scope budget");

        // (4) UNSPENT — draw-once, replay-nullified (same registry the escrow uses)
        require(!nullified[w.receiptId], "witness: replay");

        // --- ENFORCE (cursor's job, not the verifier's) ---
        leafSpent[w.leaf] += w.amount;
        nullified[w.receiptId] = true;
        emit CursorAdvanced(w.agent, w.leaf, w.amount, w.receiptId);
    }

WHY THIS SHAPE
- The witness carries BOTH the verdict (what happened, recomputably) AND the capability binding
  (which scope, how much) — so advanceCursor needs no external lookup beyond capabilityRoot.
- valid AND matches AND leaf-in-root AND within-subcap AND unspent is the recovery escrow's
  valid AND artifact_hash_matches AND delivery discipline, generalized to per-scope budgets.
- VERIFIER.verify() reuses our ALREADY-DEPLOYED on-chain primitive (BIP340Verifier / IReceiptVerifier,
  live on Sepolia) — no new trust assumption. The bounds layer composes ONTO the proven witness, it
  does not re-implement it.
- Shared nullifier: a draw and its layer-4 settlement nullify the SAME receiptId -> no double-spend
  across the cursor/escrow boundary.

OPEN CO-DESIGN QUESTIONS (for blockbird / the group)
1. capabilityRoot provenance: identity/agreement-scoped — bound via 8004 identity + the accepted
   8001 mandate? (root = commitment to the per-scope sub-budget tree)
2. leaf encoding: keccak(scopeId, subCap, agentId), or a richer envelope for counterparty caps
   ("A gets 3, B gets 7, max 1/counterparty")?
3. nullifier sharing: one registry shared by cursor + escrow (one draw == one settlement), or
   separate registries with an explicit link?
