this would drop into "Motivation"

Prediction market parameter validation. A prediction market resolves on an outcome but depends on the completeness of its declared input parameters. If the parameter set was incomplete — a relevant variable omitted from the committed input scope — the market can settle faithfully on a flawed input while every receipt check passes. The question "were the upfront input parameters reasonable and complete?" is the scope-contestation question: was the declared input set complete (Layer 1), and did a missing parameter matter under a pre-committed public classification function (Layer 2). An incomplete parameter set is the same structural blind spot as an agent settling on an incomplete asset set.

@DamonZwicker  reads right to me, the Layer 1 / Layer 2 mapping and the “same blind spot as an incomplete asset_set” parallel are exactly the axis. happy with it as the fourth instance.

one refinement worth a look, because it’s what makes prediction-markets earn its place next to the audit case: it’s the instance where Layer 2’s pre-committed w is load-bearing. in the audit case w is free (the PoC is the witness), so Layer 2 collapses for free — clean, but it under-shows why commit-before-outcome matters. in a market, “did the omitted variable matter to the resolution” isn’t self-evident from one artifact; the classification function has to be pinned before the outcome is known, exactly so “X separates a pair under your own committed w” stays unfalsifiable by the committer. so: audit shows the w-free collapse, prediction-markets shows why the pre-commitment discipline exists — the two instances cover both halves of Layer 2. might be worth one clause making that explicit.

it’s Jimmy’s domain though @Jimmyshi  should confirm the parameter/resolution framing matches how markets actually resolve before it’s final.

