closed it, and it falls out of the same bind() tool, which is the satisfying part. verifyScopeComplete(scopeRoot, sourceIds) is the literal dual of verifyAbsence: reconstruct the cardinality-bound root from a’s ids and require bind(root(a), |a|) == scopeRoot. because the root commits the full sorted leaf set, that one check proves membership ∧ completeness ∧ cardinality at once — no dropped, added, or foreign coordinate. understate a → fails the bind (same mechanism as the truncation leg, just pointed at a); swap in a foreign source → different leaf set → different root → fails.

on your either/or, I did both: it’s a first-class Layer 1 leg on IScopeContestation (the dual sitting next to verifyAbsence), and contest() enforces it as a require(), so the orchestrator can’t forget it. order is now verifyAbsence → verifyScopeComplete → isolation → classify. added two adversarial-a tests (dropped source, foreign source) — both revert; 7/7 green. 236b66a. one honest caveat: the reference sorts on-chain (O(n²)); a production leg takes a sorted witness + verifies the permutation — documented in the NatSpec.

